/** @page mysqloptionsguide Driver Options

    @tableofcontents

    @section mysql_sandbox Sandboxing

    In a %Qore version that checks DBI connections against the sandbox, a connection opened by code governed by a
    sandbox is checked against its network and filesystem policy before it is opened.  The driver reports the
    destination that the client library connects to: a connection without a host or with the host \c localhost (in
    lower case) goes to the UNIX socket given with the \c MYSQL_UNIX_PORT environment variable (also
    \c MARIADB_UNIX_PORT with MariaDB Connector/C) when the driver was loaded, or the library's default socket, which
    the filesystem policy decides; any other host by TCP to the port of the datasource or to port 3306.  The driver
    reads no option file, which could name another destination.  A connection handler host (\c "scheme://..."), an
    abstract UNIX socket (\c "@..."), and any connection on Windows (TCP to \c localhost, named pipes, shared
    memory) are refused with \c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED.

    A connection that was checked connects to the checked destination only: the socket is passed to the client
    library explicitly, and a host name is connected to by its checked addresses in turn (the next address only if
    the server could not be reached at the previous one), so that a name that resolves to another address later
    (also when the driver reconnects) cannot redirect it.  The client library then knows the server by its address,
    so a TLS certificate is verified against the address: with MariaDB Connector/C 3.4 or later, which verifies the
    server certificate by default, a sandboxed connection to a server whose certificate names only its host name
    (no IP address) fails (a self-signed certificate is verified with the password instead and is not affected).

    @section mysql_connect_timeout Connection establishment

    The \c connect-timeout option accepts an integer from 0 to 2147483 seconds (default 0, the native
    client default). It is applied before connecting and takes effect again on reconnection. A finite
    timeout bounds a silent server handshake according to the native client's timeout semantics.
    @par Example
    @code{.py}
    Datasource ds("mysql:app/pass@orders%db.internal:3306{connect-timeout=5}");
    ds.open();
    @endcode
    When built with MariaDB Connector/C's asynchronous API and Qore's cancellable poll API, connection
    establishment and its initial charset, autocommit and isolation commands support thread cancellation
    and program interruption without waiting for the server. The boolean constant
    \c Qore::MYSQL_HAVE_CANCELLABLE_CONNECT reports this build capability. Other client libraries use their blocking
    connection API and report a pending cancellation after that call returns; configure a finite timeout.


    When compiled again Qore 0.8.6+ the mysql driver support the following DBI options:
    - \c "collation": sets the client-side per-connection collation for string values
    - \c "optimal-numbers": return numeric types as an integer if possible, if not as an arbitrary-precision number
    - \c "string-numbers": return numeric types as strings (for backwards-compatibility)
    - \c "numeric-numbers": return numeric types as arbitrary-precision number values
    - \c "timezone": accepts a string argument that can be either a region name (ex: \c "Europe/Prague") or a UTC offset (ex: \c "+01:00") to set the server's time zone rules; this is useful if connecting to a database server in a different time zone.  If this option is not set then the server's time zone is assumed to be the same as the client's time zone; see @ref mysqltimezone.

    @note if the \c "collation" option is set, the collation is set automatically for each connection to the given
    value

    Options can be set in the \c Datasource or \c DatasourcePool constructors as in the following examples:
    @code{.py}
    Datasource ds("mysql:user/pass@db{numeric-numbers,timezone=Europe/Vienna}");
    DatasourcePool dsp("mysql:user/pass@db%host.internal:3306{optimal-numbers}");
    @endcode

    Options are stored separately for each connection.

    @section mysql_number_options Number Options

    The number options (\c "optimal-numbers", \c "string-numbers", and \c "numeric-numbers") are all mutually-exclusive; setting one automatically disables the others.   These options also ignore their arguments; the last one set takes effect (also note that setting it with any value sets the option, even \c False).

    The default if no other option is explicitly set is \c "optimal-numbers".  Note that this represents a change from previous versions where \c NUMERIC or \c DECIMAL values were returned as strings in order to avoid the loss of information.  To set the old behavior, set the \c "string-numbers" option when creating the \c Datasource or \c DatasourcePool object.
*/
